/* Physical features — Natural Earth 50m geography regions (public domain, CC0),
   filtered to what is recognisable from a cruising aircraft.

   Stored as a 1-degree cell mask, not a bounding box. A box cannot answer "am I
   over this?" for anything irregular: Greenland's box has open Atlantic in its
   south-east corner, and a London-Reykjavik flight was duly told Greenland was
   directly below. A mask covers only the ground the feature actually occupies.
   One degree is ~111 km, comfortably finer than the ~370 km horizon a feature is
   judged against.

   Island groups and geoareas are excluded entirely: Natural Earth draws those as
   region outlines rather than land, so Polynesia rasterised to 2,908 cells —
   three times the Sahara — because the polygon encloses the ocean between the
   islands. Singular islands are real land (Greenland: 661 cells, matching its
   true area) and are kept.

   Cell id = floor((lat+90)) * 360 + floor(((lon+180) mod 360)), so the
   antimeridian needs no special case. Ids are sorted, delta-encoded and written
   in base 36.
   Fields: name|class|cLon|cLat|scalerank|firstCell|deltas */
